  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NESA Center / LSE IDEAS / IISS &quot;Western Indo-Pacific Security: Concerns, Challenges &amp; Responses&quot; Workshop 01-22</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The first &quot;track-1.5&quot; Workshop on Western Indo-Pacific Security was co-hosted by the International Institute for Strategic Studies (IISS) and the foreign policy think tank of the London School of Economics and Politics (LSE IDEAS). It took place in partnership with the Near East &amp; South Asia Centre for Strategic Studies (NESA) of the US National Defence University. The Western Indo-Pacific (WIP) region - largely comprising the four sub-regions of southern and eastern Africa; the Horn of Africa and the Gulf region; the Arabian Sea region; and island territories - remains neglected within the larger geographical framework, in contrast to the central or the eastern Indo-Pacific regions. In this context, there were three objectives for the workshop: 1) To bring together the best of our academic and policy practitioners&apos; minds to unpack this important and neglected WIP region; 2) To seek better ways in which the WIP could respond to these challenges and to advance pragmatic steps toward better intra-regional cooperation and coordination; 3) To help improve western and other major powers responses to the WIP region.</narrative>
  </description>
